    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1473">

                <pb facs="1186" n="1070" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg1070"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 817.00/2207.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The American Minister to the
                            Secretary of State</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 90.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>. <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Managua</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-12-21">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">December 21, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the
                    receipt of the Department’s instruction No. 65 of the 18th ultimo (File
                    817.00/2130) together with its enclosure of a copy of the Libro Rosado of
                    Salvador for the month of September, 1912, which treats at length of the recent
                    revolution in Nicaragua.</p>
                <p>On page 2 of the Libro Rosado is printed a telegram, dated Managua, August 5,
                    1912, in which the Salvadorian Minister, Señor J. Antonio López G., reports to
                    his Government a conversation with me and alleges that he obtained my “formal
                    promise” not to disembark another man besides those who were then guarding the
                    Legation.</p>
                <p>Of course I made no such promise, formal or otherwise, and I would have
                    considered it presumption on his part to have requested any.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">George T. Weitzel.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
